&lt;p&gt;Unspecified&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id="garden-description"&gt;Garden Description&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This large house (1500m²) laid-out around a peristyle-garden is known for its mosaic pavements figuring Homeric scenes. The peristyle is entered from the S. The N wing had a vast &lt;em&gt;oecus-triclinium&lt;/em&gt; bordered on both sides by two apartments that each had two rooms and a passage to a pseudo-atrium (Plan view, Fig.1).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The peristyle was trapezoidal in shape (8.29 x 9 x 16. 15 m) and was bordered by 12 columns. Four porticos, 5m wide, enclosed the garden. The semicircular pool facing the &lt;em&gt;oecus-triclinium&lt;/em&gt;, that projected into the garden was paved with a mosaic depicting a mask of Ocean among fishes and had an inscription &amp;quot;NYMFARUM DOMUS&amp;quot; quoting a passage from Virgil's Aeneid. According to the excavator, J.-P. Darmon, a row of broken amphoras that had served as planting pots were found along the central axis of the garden facing the &lt;em&gt;oecus&lt;/em&gt; and the basin (Fig.2)&lt;/p&gt;
